# Probes that unisacc COMPILES but answers differently from the Python front
# end, one per line: <name> <reason>.  They are counted separately; a
# knownwrong that starts AGREEING is a failure, so the list cannot rot.
#
# Being on this list is worse than being refused: the C compiler produces a
# program, and the program is wrong.  That is the whole reason ccrun runs the
# answers rather than counting what compiles.
f1_ctor_dtor the expected output comes from the Python reference route, which drops __attribute__((constructor/destructor)) (wants main 0); unisacc and cc print main 12 / bye 12 -- see tests/difftest-py.knownfail
h1_block_extern the expected output comes from the Python reference route, which does not bind a block-scope extern to the file-scope object (prints 0 0 16777200 16777200); unisacc and cc print 5 11 3 7 -- see tests/difftest-py.knownfail
d1_static_constants the Python reference route rejects __builtin_offsetof and other address constants in static initializers (no output); unisacc and cc print 3 4 4 1 3 7 4 5 8
